WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.040
So who's my guest this week?

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:05.670
It's the courtroom artist, Jane Rosenberg.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:09.060
Since 1946, broadcasts and photographs

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.000
of criminal proceedings
inside U.S. Federal Courts

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.100
has been strictly banned.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.900
No matter how notorious the accused

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.610
or headline grabbing the crime,

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.890
media cameras are kept out.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.930
Instead it is left to the artist

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.640
to be the eyes of the public

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.400
and capture the drama of the courtroom.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.780
For the past 40 years,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.870
Jane Rosenberg has come face
to face with those involved

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:34.290
in some of America's
most high profile cases.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:38.070
She's sketched everyone from
El Chapo to Harvey Weinstein,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.073
and her drawings have been
seen all over the world.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.200
From the New York Courts
filled with Mob bosses

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.550
to those accused in the #MeToo
era, she has seen it all.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.430
So what is it like
drawing Ghislaine Maxwell,

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.710
Jeffrey Epstein and Bill Cosby?

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.150
Here's what's coming up.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.640
The now-convicted sex
trafficker Ghislaine Maxwell.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.050
You were obviously making eye contact.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.300
When did you realize
she was sketching you?

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.090
She had, she was leaning
on a piece of paper.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.490
She took out a piece of paper
and she was going up and down

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:09.570
looking at me, looking at me.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.870
So, you know, I knew she was sketching me,

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.420
you could tell when
somebody's sketching you.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:16.253
And is it right?

00:01:16.253 --> 00:01:17.310
I mean, I dunno if this is right,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.050
but Weinstein asked for more
hair in one of your drawings?

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.030
He did, he was walking.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.595
I was standing in an aisle seat,

00:01:25.595 --> 00:01:27.810
and he was walking out with his lawyers

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.100
and he looked over at my sketch,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.860
and he said, "Can't you gimme more hair?"

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.510
I just kept drawing and I
did not give him more hair.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.187
Jane, thank you so much for talking to me

00:01:50.187 --> 00:01:51.630
and to our viewers today.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:52.770
It's lovely to be with you.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.286
And I wonder how it does
feel to be the subject

00:01:56.286 --> 00:02:00.420
instead of the one drawing
and creating the work?

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.410
I really hate it.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.620
And that's why I don't think
cameras should be in the court.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.360
Well, no, that would
slightly put you out of work.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.140
But you've been in this line
of work for more than 40 years.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.970
Yes.
And I just wanted to ask,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.000
because the clip that has
gone, or the image, rather,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.280
I should say that's gone viral recently

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.030
is of the now-convicted sex
trafficker, Ghislaine Maxwell,

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.250
drawing you as you drew her.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.360
Yes.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.019
That didn't make me nervous.

00:02:26.019 --> 00:02:27.840
It didn't make you nervous?

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.750
No. Sketchers are fine.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.640
I think if she took out a big fat camera

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.860
and put it in my face, I'd
feel a little differently.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.260
But was that not quite an odd experience?

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.750
'Cause I imagine that
doesn't happen that often.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.010
No, that-

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.040
It did happen to me about 30 years ago

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.460
when there was, there's a
comedian named Eddie Murphy.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:48.820
I don't know if you know who he is.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:49.850
Heard of him. Yep, definitely.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:53.250
He did a little sketch
of me in a court case.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.370
He was the defendant,
or he was actually suing

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.770
or some kind of dispute with his agent,

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.020
and he was bobbing up
and down and sketching,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.930
making fun of me, you know, like pointing.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.630
He did a little sketch
of me on a little Post-it

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.310
and he gave it to me and I have it here

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:09.660
in my apartment somewhere.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.760
I found it within five years.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.860
It's still here somewhere.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.010
Okay. So once in 30 years,
it's definitely not the norm.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.360
No, it's not the norm.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.010
So it was odd.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.570
You were obviously making eye contact.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.360
When did you realize
she was sketching you?

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.120
She had, she was leaning
on a piece of paper.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.550
She took out a piece of paper
and she was going up and down

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:30.630
looking at me, looking at me.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.960
So, you know, I knew she was sketching me.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.640
You can tell when
somebody's sketching you.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.265
And it didn't put you off?

00:03:38.265 --> 00:03:42.272
It didn't really put me off,
but I felt I better get this,

00:03:42.272 --> 00:03:43.650
I better sketch this

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.810
'cause now this is what's
happening in the courtroom,

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:46.860
and I have to draw what I see.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.193
Yeah, I was just gonna say,

00:03:48.193 --> 00:03:49.800
I imagine that was pretty meta, you know,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.480
you being drawn and her drawing you.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.060
And also do you think there's a kind of,

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.460
is there a psychological
element to that, do you think?

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.690
There could be.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.050
A lot of people are
throwing in their opinions

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.930
about what it means and
what it meant to her,

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.057
that she was trying to like have power.

00:04:06.057 --> 00:04:09.690
I don't really know. I don't read minds.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.430
I can't figure out what she was doing,

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.440
but I had to draw her
and I was really happy

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.740
she was looking straight
ahead as you may be right now,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:20.070
because a lot of times I'm
seated right behind a defendant

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.530
and I can't see anything
but the back of their head.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:25.020
So if I can get a front view, it's great.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.390
A lot of times photographers
outside of a courthouse

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.625
will shout out somebody's name

00:04:29.625 --> 00:04:31.470
when they're trying to get their image,

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.230
just so that they get
them to turn front view.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.730
So it was an important thing for me,

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.700
and I wanted to keep it
going as long as possible.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:39.721
It was great.

00:04:39.721 --> 00:04:40.554
Well, that's interesting as well.

00:04:40.554 --> 00:04:42.150
I mean, did you get to see what she drew?

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.650
Did she leave it on the table?

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.020
I did go running out.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.360
It was a break and I went
out and ran after her lawyer.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.510
I said, "Can I see? I'd like to know

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:52.589
what was she sketching?"

00:04:52.589 --> 00:04:54.330
And she said, "You know, Jane,

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.060
I can't discuss that with you."

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.170
So I really don't know. I never saw it.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:04:59.805
You're of course drawing as well,

00:04:59.805 --> 00:05:02.760
and it has been a remarkable
time with the pandemic

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.850
where you have a lot of
people's faces now obscured

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:06.870
with the mask.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:07.863
How has that been?

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.570
It's partly easier and partly harder.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.820
It depends on who the person,

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.670
Ghislaine Maxwell had
great eyebrows and eyes

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:18.900
and good shape hair

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.940
because that's all I've got to work with.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.870
It made it harder for me to
sketch because I use pastels

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.210
and I'm used to blowing
some of the dust off.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:28.043
I'd be going,

00:05:28.957 --> 00:05:29.790
but then it's in my mask,

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.220
so it was hard for me
to wear a mask and work,

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.443
but drawing everybody else in a mask,

00:05:34.443 --> 00:05:37.530
it makes, it's not as
interesting, I'm sure,

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:41.550
to make a portrait of
somebody with a mask on,

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.550
but I had to draw what I saw.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.380
Well some have also felt, you know,

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.600
it's hidden people's faces at the time

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.010
where you do normally get,

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.936
so even going into the
court, some people have felt,

00:05:52.936 --> 00:05:56.523
it's been helpful to those
who are on trial in some way.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:01.230
I think it's been hard
for a defendant's team,

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:03.360
the lawyers to pick a jury,

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.900
because you can't see their
faces, it's hard to read.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.880
You wanna pick a certain type, you know,

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.130
when you're trying to select a jury.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.781
I think it really made it
hard for the them to...

00:06:13.781 --> 00:06:17.010
People look different when
they take their mask off.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.040
I was working with a
reporter with a mask on,

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.350
and then I saw them outside with no mask.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:24.180
I had no idea that was the same person.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.120
No, I know. I've had that
exact same experience myself,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:27.960
quite a lot of times.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.580
And I just wanna see and check

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:32.070
that I've actually met that person before.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:33.750
With this particular image,

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.760
'cause it really did captivate people.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.073
I know you are not
actually on social media,

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.070
but this really did go viral.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:44.790
I know you can keep the
images as well that you draw,

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.970
you submit a scan of
them or you give a copy,

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:49.470
and then you keep the original.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.270
Have you been offered money?

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:53.100
Oh yeah. Sure. Yes.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:53.933
Are, are you gonna sell it?

00:06:53.933 --> 00:06:56.070
No, I'm not selling it, yet.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:07:01.070
I have a collection of 40,
almost 43 years of sketches,

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:02.010
thousands of them.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.260
And there's certain precious ones

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:06.810
that I think are more important
to me in my collection

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.370
to make it all worth whatever.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:11.013
And I don't know, I'm
not ready to sell it.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:13.860
Just about something
you were saying though

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:17.220
with Ghislaine, which is I
think speaks to your work

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.140
is that you've gotta draw what you see

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:20.490
and it's obvious that you have had

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.980
to take the emotion out of it.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.540
Take my emotion out of it.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:25.373
Yeah, exactly.

00:07:25.373 --> 00:07:26.700
You have to-
Because if somebody else

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:28.895
shows emotion, I do have to put that in.

00:07:28.895 --> 00:07:30.493
If somebody's crying,
Of course.

00:07:30.493 --> 00:07:31.860
I have to capture their emotion,

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:35.520
but I have to try to not let my feelings

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:36.810
go into the drawing,

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:40.800
which sometimes I do have
reactions on some cases.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.430
Sometimes I feel like, I cry sometimes,

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:48.750
and you know, tears are
really bad for pastels.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.360
I just try to be neutral and journalistic.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.310
I witness some horrific cases

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:56.490
and I see horrible crime scene photos.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:59.490
Like I just did a Derek Chauvin case.

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.760
I watched that tape over and
over because it was shown

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.620
from several different angles.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:06.839
And it was really hard for me to do that.

00:08:06.839 --> 00:08:08.520
I had to look.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.050
I had to look, I had to draw it.

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:11.040
I couldn't look away.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.770
People have an instinct to look away

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:15.060
from a horrific scene like that,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.190
but I had to really study the face.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.050
It was just horrible to watch somebody die

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.790
over and over and over.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:23.190
What impact has your work had on you

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.680
in your life outside the court?

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.680
I think, I do painting as well

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.440
and my subject matter is not crime,

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.370
and it's just cityscapes

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:37.530
of just more pleasant things, I think.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:42.210
You know, I don't wanna see or
watch any crime scene movies.

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.910
I just need to escape it.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:46.770
I don't touch pastels when I leave court.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:50.850
I only will use oils in my painting.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:54.210
No, 'cause I imagine some of
what you've been exposed to,

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:55.470
I dunno if it's made you,

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.840
has it changed the way
you feel towards people?

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.690
Do you sometimes think about
the worst things you've seen

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.330
and heard and maybe
struggle to trust people

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:06.063
or think of the bad things people can do?

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.390
I try. It's hard.

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:13.560
People's faces don't,

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.740
as I was saying to somebody
about Bernie Madoff,

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.110
he looked like such a kind old gentleman.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.630
It would be so easy to
just fall prey to him

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:23.880
and like give him all
your money and trust him.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.837
You can't really always
tell by somebody's face

00:09:26.837 --> 00:09:29.760
what they're really like inside.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.670
That's maybe something
I've learned from trials,

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:33.592
watching people.

00:09:33.592 --> 00:09:35.730
You can't always tell.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.400
There's brilliant con artists out there

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.520
who just can mask their true self

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.720
and you just have to be careful.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:48.233
Have you ever been frightened in court?

00:09:48.233 --> 00:09:51.840
I was once, I was
sketching a group of the,

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.477
I think they were the embassy bombers.

00:09:53.477 --> 00:09:55.500
It was a group of terrorists,
and there were a lot of them

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:57.900
and they sat them all inside the jury box,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.270
'cause they had nowhere else to put them.

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:02.550
And they let me sit in the well,

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:06.848
which is where the tables
for the lawyers would be.

00:10:06.848 --> 00:10:10.380
And I was sketching, and
one of the defendants

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.780
jumped out of the well, lept over.

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:13.613
It was sort of like,

00:10:13.613 --> 00:10:16.350
I think I had just seen a
Tarzan movie with my son

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:17.910
and he leapt over with both arms,

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.250
took his feet and started
running across the courtroom.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:22.890
And the judge stood up and
pressed the panic button

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:24.960
and the marshals were standing in the back

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.870
and started running up and tackled the guy

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:29.850
against the wall right next to me.

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.200
It was terrifying.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.450
I got on the floor, I held my pastel box,

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:34.530
I was really afraid,

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.990
which is resting on a
little tripod next to me.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:38.700
I did not want that to go on the floor,

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.160
and I just squatted down
on the floor next to that.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.685
So that was a pretty odd moment.

00:10:44.685 --> 00:10:46.320
Well, I mean, if you are
regularly in the room

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:47.580
with big criminals,

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:50.610
something at some point
I'm sure is gonna go down.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.500
That was the time that it went down.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:54.813
And I had never been allowed in

00:10:54.813 --> 00:10:57.030
to sit where I was sitting again.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:59.833
They always, then it was just a time

00:10:59.833 --> 00:11:02.040
where they just were extra cautious

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:05.640
since that point about
where they let anybody sit.

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:08.430
What's it like sitting
opposite household names?

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:12.690
I'm thinking R. Kelly and to
an extent Harvey Weinstein.

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.130
Okay. Household names.

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.410
These are all sex predators.

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:19.020
So it's, the Me Too era,

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.450
of a lot of the cases I've been covering

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:23.370
there's so many Me Too's.

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:24.420
What's it like?

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.760
I don't know, I think
they're all perverted

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:27.810
in different ways.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:31.110
Like Weinstein, you know,
they all, there's just,

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:34.500
I think when people have a
lot of money, power and fame,

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:39.500
it corrupts them and they
just become deviants.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:41.807
And is it right?

00:11:41.807 --> 00:11:42.780
I mean, I dunno if this is right,

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:47.550
but Weinstein asked for more
hair in one of your drawings?

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:49.080
Oh yeah, he did.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.160
He was walking.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:51.739
I was sitting in an aisle seat,

00:11:51.739 --> 00:11:53.970
and he was walking out with his lawyers

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:55.230
and he looked over at my sketch,

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:57.484
and he said, "Can't you gimme more hair?"

00:11:57.484 --> 00:12:00.810
I just kept drawing, and I
did not give him more hair.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:03.810
No, I imagine as a woman, and also someone

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:06.390
who just heard the
details you'd just heard,

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.240
but he's also still gonna be
someone who's used to power,

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:12.273
that that is not something you
would respond to pretty well.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:15.570
He's not gonna have power over me,

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:18.210
so I did not give him more hair,

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:20.460
although many, many years ago,

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:23.760
John Gotti did ask me to
like shave off his chin,

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:26.640
a little double chin, and I did do that.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:28.500
Oh, why were you scared of him?

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:29.523
Scared of him?

00:12:30.719 --> 00:12:35.250
It was, I don't think I
was directly afraid of him,

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:39.870
but there were a lot
of Mob trials back then

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:40.920
in the eighties.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:42.330
A lot of 'em.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:46.355
I was surrounded by a lot of

00:12:46.355 --> 00:12:49.440
Mafia people back then.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:52.500
Trial after trial after,
that was like Me Too now,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:56.613
back then it was Mafia
trials, Commission Trial,

00:12:57.510 --> 00:13:01.260
John Gotti, the Pizza Connection Trial.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.732
There was, I can't even
remember, they went,

00:13:03.732 --> 00:13:05.160
there were constantly,

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.020
there were five different
families in New York

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:10.380
and there were so many trials
and they were switching-

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.030
I couldn't even follow it anymore.

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:14.663
They were switching power, like Gotti had

00:13:14.663 --> 00:13:16.590
during the Commission Trial,

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.930
one of the defendants there had vanished

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:23.930
'cause Gotti had taken him
out and then he rose to power.

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:26.460
That was a whole different era.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:27.293
It's interesting

00:13:27.293 --> 00:13:28.950
that there will have
been phases like that.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.290
The Mobs, now #MeToo.

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:33.960
Yes, that seems to be
the way the news works.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:37.500
It's not, I hardly, I can't even think

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:41.190
of a Mafia trial I had covered recently,

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:43.020
maybe six years ago.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:44.220
Nobody seems to care anymore.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.830
Now it's #MeToo, #MeToo, #MeToo.

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.950
There was an era of financial crimes.

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:52.770
There was, you know, Martha
Stewart and Bernie Madoff.

00:13:52.770 --> 00:13:55.773
And there were a lot of
financial crimes for a while.

00:13:56.761 --> 00:14:00.210
And terrorists was a
big theme for a while.

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:01.890
And have you ever, I mean,

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:05.070
a lot of the people you've
mentioned don't get out of jail.

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:06.330
Some of them will, of course.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:08.523
Have you ever met them in real life?

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:10.980
Once they get out of jail,

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.980
they probably serve such a
long sentence that I don't,

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:17.820
no, I've never met them
after their trials.

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:19.530
I'm usually onto the next trial.

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:22.350
How long do you have to make the drawing?

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:25.560
That all depends on if
it's an arraignment,

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:29.580
somebody during the Boston
Marathon bombing arraignment,

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:31.500
he was there for six minutes.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:32.520
People timed it.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:33.720
I usually have no idea,

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.550
'cause I lose track of
time and everything.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:38.790
I'm just so, arraignments go very quickly.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.620
And in six minutes I have to get

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.750
as much information down as I can

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:46.410
and then flush it out from
my memory, finish it up.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:49.003
I can't obviously complete
it in six minutes,

00:14:49.003 --> 00:14:52.140
but I have to have as much
information as I can get.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:54.270
And sometimes I have a witness,

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.760
the same witness if I'm on a trial.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:00.330
They may be on the stand
for days, the same person.

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:02.400
So I have a lot more time.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.070
I might do a wide shot
with a lot of people in it,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:05.910
take my time.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:08.970
I might do a wide shot, a tight shot.

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:09.810
It varies.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:13.290
So everything depends on
what I'm covering that day

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:14.610
and how much time I've got.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:17.370
And early on I believe you
drew one of the first people

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:20.070
to be executed by
electric chair in Alabama

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:24.060
when it reintroduced the
death penalty, John Evans.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:26.583
What was that like and
how did it impact you?

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.013
It was pretty awful.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:32.790
The equipment malfunctioned.

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:34.590
Flames came out of the knee pad,

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:36.210
they came out of the helmet,

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:39.660
and I think they had
to redo it a few times.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:41.820
I felt, I looked around the-

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.470
you don't see who pulls the switch.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:46.500
And I look around the room
and nobody's, you know,

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:48.150
people are hands behind their back.

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:51.210
I felt very responsible, like I did it.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.130
My hands felt dirty.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:55.080
And then I remember driving home

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.180
during a thunder and lightning storm.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:01.020
I had to go another hour away
to get to where I was staying,

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:04.020
and driving through puddles,
torrential rainstorm,

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:07.620
and I felt like this is a
message from the higher power.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:10.830
And I felt like I don't
wanna be part of murder.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:12.270
I think murder is wrong.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.580
I don't think the government
should be doing murder,

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:15.960
and I don't wanna do it.

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:17.910
I don't believe in the death penalty.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:19.953
So that was that in
terms of those drawings?

00:16:19.953 --> 00:16:22.740
That was the only time I
covered an electrocution,

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:23.850
and yeah.

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:24.683
Wow.

00:16:24.683 --> 00:16:26.365
You know, there are other horrible things,

00:16:26.365 --> 00:16:29.850
as I just told you about
Derek Chauvin trial,

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:31.980
that was pretty hard to watch, and-

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:34.230
And I suppose being in New York as well,

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:36.150
there's a particular, as you've described,

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:38.520
there's a particular level
of crime at times, you know,

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:41.250
with the Mafia, a lot of violence,

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:43.050
there's been a lot that you've had to see.

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:44.790
And then you also have the celebrities

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:47.580
because a lot of the time,
this is where this happens.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:51.660
Well, New York City is running a-

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:55.443
violence going on all the
time now, more than ever.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:58.230
That's why I'm not plain air painting

00:16:58.230 --> 00:16:59.760
on the streets of the city anymore.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:02.040
I don't feel comfortable
standing out there

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:04.980
putting an easel up and
getting lost in an easel.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:06.750
I don't know who's
gonna come up behind me.

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:09.240
There's a lot of
emotionally disturbed people

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:13.110
and there's a lot of violence,
and it's just horrific.

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:17.370
And the city is changing
and it needs to get fixed.

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:20.010
It's pretty bad right now, New York City.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:22.410
So the trials I'm seeing, I feel safe.

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:27.410
I'm usually there's a lot of
court officers or marshals

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:30.660
or CSOs standing around.

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:32.520
I feel like I'm safe to draw there.

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:34.260
It's not like sitting on a subway

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.570
where somebody could get
angry if I'm sketching them.

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:38.490
Nobody's gonna come attack me.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:39.750
You went viral again.

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:41.340
I mean, maybe you've
been viral lots of times

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:43.650
and you don't know because
you're not on social media,

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:45.960
but there was also another time,

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:48.450
which I think is smart a lot of the time,

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.790
because of your depiction of Tom Brady,

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:52.920
the American footballer in court,

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:54.150
over a cheating accusation,

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:57.495
people saying that your
picture didn't look like him.

00:17:57.495 --> 00:17:59.130
I mean, that's not the same, of course,

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:02.520
as having art critics look
at your art for art's sake,

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:04.920
it's a kind of different lens on it.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.680
But does that upset you?
Does that bother you?

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:11.220
It did back then a lot,
but I think I learned,

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:15.060
I had a lot of emails
and there's fans who,

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:17.430
it depended what team you're rooting for.

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:18.960
'Cause some people loved the sketch

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:21.873
and some people like, how
could I make him look so ugly?

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.350
You know, I don't tend to flatter people

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:28.140
when I'm doing quick courtroom sketches.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:29.010
Well, I'd imagine

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.200
that not a lot of them
look their best either.

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:32.610
It's their day in court.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:33.443
That's right.

00:18:33.443 --> 00:18:35.640
They just come in, the fluorescent lights,

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:38.970
and they're like, "Get this
over with. Get me outta here."

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:41.970
Have you ever been surprised
by a famous person coming in

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:43.020
who doesn't actually really look

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:44.940
like you thought they would?

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:47.490
I just at the moment
remembered Uma Thurman

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:49.230
coming to court, who's very beautiful.

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.840
And she sat down in the witness box

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:55.350
and nobody would ever think
the sketch I made was her,

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:58.350
because you know, she's
not looking glamorous

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:00.537
and I'm sure there've been a lot of these.

00:19:00.537 --> 00:19:02.670
I just can't remember them.

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:04.920
You've mentioned around your work

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:08.550
and how important it is to
capture what's going on.

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.220
And for you, I can tell
it's a very important part

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:14.070
of the justice system and transparency.

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:15.300
But what do you say to those

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.970
who think it's time for cameras?

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.280
Well, as I said in the beginning,

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.170
I don't really like being on camera.

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:24.600
I'm not comfortable with it.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:27.840
I think it gives a lot of
power in an editing room

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:29.190
to the media.

00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:30.450
They can make-

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:31.590
people don't-

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:32.910
when they see something on camera,

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:34.380
they believe it's the truth.

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:36.210
It's a photo. They don't lie.

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:39.240
But there is power in the
way things are edited.

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:42.390
Things can be shown, clips
of certain expressions

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:44.370
that may not show the truth.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:48.037
When a sketch is shown, I
don't think people have that,

00:19:48.037 --> 00:19:50.490
"Oh, this is the truth. This is where-"

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:53.250
It just captures a feeling
of what's going on.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:57.270
So I don't, I think you have to watch out

00:19:57.270 --> 00:19:58.590
with cameras in the court.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:01.470
They may be quieter now,
they're not as obtrusive.

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:04.170
They don't make loud
clicking noises anymore.

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:08.190
All that may be true, but what is true?

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:09.540
What is the truth?

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.000
Well, I mean your, your
country, where I am,

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.260
this has been a big thing.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:16.470
What is truth, post-truth
era, those sorts of things.

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.000
I suppose it's one of
those debates, isn't it?

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:19.710
And I'm interested as
well, as a journalist,

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:23.430
because of transparency in
courts and how we best do that.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:25.523
Well, courtrooms are open to the public.

00:20:25.523 --> 00:20:28.890
People are allowed to
come in and watch trials.

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:32.970
They're not sealed off
unless it's a sealed case.

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:37.970
And if a cameras running 24-7

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:41.730
in the courtroom, maybe that
will give more information.

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:45.270
But TV news tends to edit clips

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:49.140
of what they think is the
story they wanna tell.

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:52.297
And if the judge is yawning
they may show that and say,

00:20:52.297 --> 00:20:54.390
"Oh, he's not paying attention."

00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:56.850
And you know, anything could happen.

00:20:56.850 --> 00:21:01.050
And I think there probably
are witnesses like me

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:03.690
who don't feel comfortable
in front of a camera.

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:06.510
Yeah. And also victims of course, as well.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:09.480
Victims, of course, I can't
even sketch their likeness,

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:10.920
'cause I'm not permitted.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:12.750
Not permitted to sketch their face.

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:17.370
So I have to, during Ghislaine
Maxwell and Weinstein

00:21:17.370 --> 00:21:19.950
and plenty of cases,

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:24.510
I've had to draw featureless faces.

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:29.510
So I'd get the gesture,
maybe the tears, hands, hair.

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:31.702
But no, I can't really,

00:21:31.702 --> 00:21:34.380
I can't let their whole likeness show

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:37.320
where somebody could pick them
out and say, "Oh, I know."

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:39.960
And jurors, I can't show their face too.

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:41.638
Especially anonymous jurors.

00:21:41.638 --> 00:21:43.710
I mean, in the case of Ghislaine Maxwell,

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:47.700
bringing it back up, a lot
of people make a big thing

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:52.200
of what the person looks like
when they're found guilty.

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:54.633
People wanna read into
that moment, don't they?

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:57.690
Do you draw that exact moment?

00:21:57.690 --> 00:21:59.070
That response to the verdict?

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:01.470
I'm watching what's going on, and I-

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:04.110
if she broke down crying, yeah.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:05.640
I would draw that moment.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:08.910
But she didn't really have
any reaction at that moment

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:09.743
that I could see.

00:22:09.743 --> 00:22:10.576
Is that quite common?

00:22:10.576 --> 00:22:12.183
Yes. It's common.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:14.670
People don't always react.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:17.790
Maybe they're in shock, I don't know.

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:20.280
Yeah. What a moment, what
many moments you've been in,

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:24.323
in people's lives recording the
biggest moment in many ways,

00:22:24.323 --> 00:22:26.610
often for the very worst reasons.

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:29.160
But sometimes they are
exonerated as well, of course,

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:31.830
and that will be a very
important moment as well.

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:35.580
Speaking of Mafia trials,
the last one I did,

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:39.450
he was exonerated, and the
courtroom erupted with his fans,

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:40.950
like cheering, "Yay!"

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.380
And they ran out celebrating.

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:46.800
So that's rare though, yeah.

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:48.630
Jane, if you, could do it all again,

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:50.280
do you think you'd go
into this line of work,

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:51.810
knowing what you know about it?

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:54.600
Yes. I do love what I do.

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:55.920
I think I'm very lucky.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:57.600
I love drawing people.

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:58.740
I'm very lucky I found a way

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:01.560
to earn a living doing it and feel safe,

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:05.100
and I love it and I would
do it all over again, yes.

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:06.780
Well, I've loved talking to you.

00:23:06.780 --> 00:23:10.110
Thank you so much for letting us in

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.864
and letting me and us
turn the camera onto you

00:23:12.864 --> 00:23:14.760
and focus on you for a bit,

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:16.710
but we'll let you get back
to focusing on others.

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:17.550
Thank you so much.

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:18.900
Okay.

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:20.910
And thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:24.183
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

